```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is the ospfd process not running on router_core_1. All other FRR
daemons (zebra, mgmtd, staticd) are active and the physical interfaces are
fully operational — connected routes for 172.16.0.0/31 (eth0→router_core_2),
172.16.0.2/31 (eth1→router_core_3), and 172.16.0.6/31 (eth2→switch_dist_1_1)
are all present, which directly disproves the networking team's claim of a
physical cabling problem.

The /etc/frr/frr.conf on router_core_1 contains a correct OSPF configuration
(router-id 172.16.0.0, eth0/eth1 in area 0, eth2 in area 1), but because
ospfd was never started, this config is never loaded. The running config is
therefore an empty stub with no router ospf block. With ospfd down,
router_core_1 forms no OSPF adjacencies (confirmed: router_core_2's neighbor
table shows only router_core_3 and switch_dist_2_1 — no entry for
router_core_1). As a result, router_core_1 has no route to the server farm
(10.200.0.0/24) and cannot act as the ABR between backbone area 0 and area 1.
switch_dist_1_1, which sits in area 1 behind router_core_1 and is the
first-hop gateway (10.1.1.1) for host_1_1_1_1, also has no inter-area route
to 10.200.0.0/24 — so packets from host_1_1_1_1 are dropped at the
distribution layer before ever reaching the core.

Root cause: ospfd is not running on router_core_1, silently disabling OSPF on
the ABR that bridges area 1 (access/distribution) to the backbone.

Fix: Enable ospfd on router_core_1 by setting ospfd=yes in /etc/frr/daemons,
then restart FRR (service frr restart). The frr.conf is already correct; no
routing configuration changes are needed.
=== END ===
```